    array.push(cH, "Warsh took the stage and talked about AI, not rates, so the dollar keeps its lead over the euro.|The pound is still waiting for its jobs report, and Warsh gave the greenback no reason to slow down.|No rate hints from Warsh, no words from Tokyo: the gap holds at +5.|Switzerland's economy grew a little in Q3, which neither helps nor hurts the franc's case.|Canada's inflation came in softer than expected, and the loonie's case got a little weaker.|The Aussie had a silent Monday, and the dollar got no new push either.|The kiwi spent Monday waiting for milk prices, while the Fed chair talked about artificial intelligence.|Two European currencies, two quiet days, and a lean that did not move.|The yen's soft spot from the morning is still there, and the euro did nothing to exploit it.|Swiss growth came in modestly positive, and the tie at 0 stays a tie.|The Aussie kept its edge on a day with no Australian news at all.|No news from Paris or Wellington, so the −4 lean simply carries over.|Canada's soft inflation tilted this stand-off a touch toward the euro, but not enough to pick a side.|The yen is still nursing its GDP miss; the pound is still waiting for its turn.|Swiss GDP grew modestly, and the franc's haven role stayed asleep.|Both sides spent Monday waiting for Tuesday.|Level at +1 each, with both currencies' first big tests now less than a day away.|Canada's soft CPI handed the pound a little more room in this matchup.|A quiet Aussie and a still-wobbly yen keep this lean just above the line.|Carry beats a modest Swiss growth number.|Neither neighbour had news, so the +2 tie stays.|Canada's CPI came in cool, and the Aussie's lead over the loonie edged wider on paper.|The kiwi and the yen both had a quiet afternoon, and the stand-off holds at +1.|Swiss growth stayed positive, the kiwi stayed patient, and the +4 lean survived.|Softer Canadian inflation gave the kiwi a slightly bigger cushion.|Both legs are softer today, and they pull this gap in opposite directions.|Canada's soft inflation nudged this stand-off slightly toward the franc.|The franc got a modest growth print; the yen got no help from Tokyo.|Gold touched a new low for the year, but the model's gold factor did not move.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED~Warsh's AI/productivity speech gave no rate guidance; Dec 25bp priced 94% (6% for 50bp); Collins/Schmid/Jefferson hawkish.|CONFIRMED~No OAT–Bund reading after 13 Nov (151.8bp); Senate PLFSS spending debate continues; no relief signal.|CONFIRMED~No verified BoE item; Pill speech flagged but text unpublished; UK jobs Tue 09:00, CPI Wed 09:00 Bucharest.|WEAKENED (watch)~Q3 GDP miss carried: shadow R3 −1, raw −0.9 held at 0; no MoF/BoJ comment verified today.|CONFIRMED~Swiss Q3 GDP flash +0.2% q/q (industry up, services flat); no consensus, no surprise; SNB silent.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Oct CPI 3.1% vs 3.2% exp., m/m 0.2% vs 0.3%, trim 1.8%, median 2.0%: shadow R3 −1, raw −4.5 held at −4.|CONFIRMED~No Australian or RBA item today; RBA minutes Tue 02:30 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED~No NZ or RBNZ item today; GDT auction Tue 14:00 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED · fragile (gap −3)~Gold factor −3 unchanged; gold at a year low $3,855 (price context); no ETF/central-bank data; 10y 5.14%.")
