    array.push(cH, "Wall Street had a rough Monday, yet the euro could not use the dollar's wobble because Paris got worse.|Pill's hawkish words reached us in full, and the pound now walks into its jobs report with a December hike in its pocket.|The yen got two pieces of help overnight: a market scare and firmer BoJ hike bets.|The fear gauge jumped, and the franc's alarm is starting to ring, though not loudly enough yet.|Oil fell another 3.5% and the loonie's day got even worse.|The RBA minutes sounded tough, but the Aussie still had the worst night in the G10.|The kiwi walked into a risk-off night with no news of its own to lean on.|Paris widened, London talked tough: the lean toward the pound got a little firmer, though the score stays.|The yen's weak spot from Monday is healing, while the euro's French bruise got darker.|Both sides of this tie lost a little overnight, so it stays a tie.|The Aussie stumbled overnight, but its six-point lead over the euro is too wide to lose in one night.|A worse day for France than for New Zealand keeps the kiwi in front.|Cheap oil and a wider French spread hit both sides, and the stand-off survives.|The pound got a hawkish voice and the yen got firmer BoJ bets: still level.|Sterling found a hawkish ally, but rising market fear is the risk to watch here.|The Aussie slipped overnight; the pound's chance to tip the scale comes at 09:00.|Two tie-breakers land today, five hours apart.|Oil kept falling and the pound kept its hawks: this lean got a little more room.|This is the pair where the overnight scare matters most.|High yield versus zero yield, on a night when fear came back a little.|Both Antipodeans felt the risk-off night, so the gap stays +2.|Even after a soft night for the Aussie, the loonie's oil problem is bigger.|Risk-off nights usually favour the yen over the kiwi, but the scores did not move.|A nervous market is the franc's best friend, so this lean is worth watching today.|Dairy has not spoken yet, but oil already did, and it was bad news for the loonie.|Cheap oil, firmer BoJ bets and a risk scare all point the same way here.|Oil weighed on the loonie and fear helped the franc, but the stand-off holds.|Two havens on a nervous night: the yen got the extra help.|Markets got scared on Monday, and gold still fell.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED~Equities −1.9%, VIX 22.91, yet Dec 25bp still 91% priced (50bp 9%); retail sales today 15:30 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~OAT–Bund widened to 155.0bp on 16 Nov (151.8bp on 13 Nov), further from the 137bp relief level.|CONFIRMED~Pill (FT, 14 Nov) backs a December hike, matching 73% pricing; UK jobs 09:00, CPI Wed 09:00 Bucharest.|CONFIRMED (watch)~BoJ Dec hike 85% priced (MUFG, one source) after the GDP miss; shadow R3 −1, raw −0.9 held at 0.|CONFIRMED (watch)~VIX 22.91 nears the franc's 25 trigger but is below it; no SNB item.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Brent −3.55% to $92.44, $91.8 in Asia; CPI miss carried: shadow raw −4.5 held at −4.|WEAKENED (watch)~RBA minutes split 6–3 for the hike, peak priced ( 22bp cuts by end-2027); risk scare partial (VIX 22.9).|CONFIRMED~No verified NZ or RBNZ item overnight; GDT auction 14:00 Bucharest today.|CONFIRMED · fragile (gap −3)~Gold −0.86% to $3,853 on a risk-off day (price context); gold factor −3 unchanged; 10y 5.12%.")
