    array.push(cH, "Williams closed the door on a jumbo hike, yet the dollar still walked into Wednesday holding the extra notch.|Sterling's big test lands at 09:00, and the greenback arrives at it with Williams' blessing for more, but smaller, hikes.|The pair hovered just under 160 all night while Tokyo kept its warnings in the drawer.|The franc's alarm finally rang: the fear gauge closed above 25.|Oil settled a touch higher, but the loonie still carries its soft-inflation bruise into the minutes.|Australian wages jumped, yet the tipped stand-off stays tipped.|The kiwi slid to its weakest level since 2020 on the charts, but its model score did not budge.|Two verdicts arrive today for this cross: UK inflation at breakfast and French senators later on.|Three points of distance, and both sides spent the night saying nothing.|The franc's fear trigger fired at Tuesday's close, but in a tie that only matters if Friday agrees.|A wage surprise in Sydney gave the Aussie side of this matchup fresh support.|The kiwi's chart looks grim, but this cross is judged on fundamentals, and those did not change.|Oil's small settle gain and France's quiet night leave this cross without a direction.|The pound gets its answer at 09:00; the yen waits until Friday.|The franc's comeback condition was met on paper, just as the pound heads into its inflation test.|Australia moved first with a wage surprise; Britain answers with inflation this morning.|Level at +1 each, and only one of them has a catalyst today.|Two routes to a bigger lean, and one of them could open at 09:00.|Strong wages on one side, a risk scare on the other: the classic carry cross is pulled both ways.|High yield versus safe haven, and Tuesday's close tilted the board toward the haven.|Across the Tasman, only one side had news, and it was good news for Canberra.|A wage beat in Australia and a quiet loonie keep this commodity-currency lean healthy.|A sliding kiwi and a silent Tokyo: the stand-off survives another night.|The VIX crossed the franc's line at Tuesday's close, and the kiwi had no news to push back.|The kiwi's slide on the charts does not change the fact that the loonie's fundamentals are weaker.|Both legs are held by the one-point rule, and the night brought nothing to break the hold.|The franc's trigger fired at the close, which matters in this tie only if Friday confirms it.|Two havens, and Tuesday's fear spike favoured the one that was losing.|Gold printed a fresh 14-month low in Asia, but its gap is set by inputs, not by the price.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED (strengthened)~Williams: no 'dramatic action' needed (no 50bp), more hikes likely; 25bp December 98%. Shadow R3 +2, raw 6.3 → +6.|CONFIRMED~No OAT–Bund reading after 155.0bp (16 Nov); Senate PLFSS vote today (time not verified).|CONFIRMED (mixed data)~No BoE item overnight; +1 central, +2 if the labour data are scored hawkish. UK CPI 09:00 today.|CONFIRMED (watch)~No MoF/BoJ comment overnight; USD/JPY 159.4; exports up a 14th month (not scored). Raw −0.9 held at 0.|INVALIDATED (bullish condition met, mode~VIX closed 25.46 on 17 Nov: the 'VIX above 25' condition is met; regime re-measured only from Friday closes, so shadow stays −3.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Brent settled $93.14 (+0.76%), $92.6 in Asia; no Canadian release. Raw −4.5 held at −4.|WEAKENED (watch)~Q3 wages +1.1% q/q vs 1.0% exp (3.5% y/y): R3 shadow +1, raw 3.6 held at +3; risk scare (VIX >25) offsets.|CONFIRMED~No NZ data or RBNZ item overnight; kiwi at its lowest since 2020 is price context only.|CONFIRMED · fragile (gap −3)~G −3 unchanged; XAU shadow = G + USD shadow = +3. Fresh low near $3,825 in Asia is context only.")
