    array.push(cH, "The Fed's own minutes said what the speakers have been saying all week: more hikes are coming.|A cooler UK inflation print yesterday, a hawkish set of Fed minutes last night: both nudges point the same way.|Hawkish minutes gave the dollar side another push, while Tokyo stayed with words rather than action.|The fear gauge slipped back under 25, so the franc's comeback trigger is no longer live, only close.|Oil slid again and the Fed minutes leaned hawkish: two winds blowing in the dollar's favour.|Australia lost 43,500 jobs in October, and the stand-off is now leaning clearly toward the dollar.|No news from Wellington again, while the Fed minutes kept the dollar's engine running.|France cleared one Senate hurdle, Britain is still digesting softer inflation, and the lean has not moved.|A Senate vote for the euro and a fresh warning for the yen, but neither moved this three-point lean.|With the VIX back under 25, the franc's route to a lean in this tie has closed for now.|The Aussie's jobs report collapsed overnight, and this euro-weak lean just lost some of its footing.|Only one side of this cross had news, and it did not help the euro.|Cheaper oil and a wider French spread: both legs got a little heavier, and the tie stays a tie.|The pound came out of its data run weaker, the yen held steady, and neither has a lead.|Both legs are on watch: a softer pound after CPI and a franc whose fear trigger just went quiet.|Australia's jobs shock lands in a pair that was already a stand-off.|Two currencies at +1 and a quiet night for both.|Oil sank again for the loonie, but the pound's own softness keeps this lean under watch.|The carry currency's labour market cracked overnight, and this three-point lean is now hanging by a thread.|High yield met a weak jobs report, while the haven's trigger cooled just below 25.|The Australian jobs report was the one release this cross was waiting for, and it went against the Aussie.|Both commodity currencies took a hit: jobs for the Aussie, oil for the loonie.|Wellington silent, Tokyo repeating itself: the stand-off continues.|The franc's fear trigger switched off at Wednesday's close, but only by a whisker.|Oil kept sliding, and that is the loonie's problem in this matchup.|Two legs held in place by rounding, and the night did not break either hold.|The franc's trigger cooled and the loonie's oil weighed: still no winner.|A haven duel where both havens have soft spots this morning.|Gold fell 3.5% after hawkish minutes, but its gap is set by inputs, and those did not change.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED (strengthened)~FOMC minutes: nearly all officials see more hikes as likely appropriate; Waller backs considering 50bp; December 25bp priced 90–95%.|CONFIRMED~Senate adopted the PLFSS 201–124 (deficit €14bn vs €12.7bn); OAT–Bund widened to 156.3bp on 17 Nov; CMP 26 Nov.|WEAKENED (watch)~No new BoE item; carried from CPI 3.6% below the BoE 3.7% projection (central +1, dovish R3 scenario still +1).|CONFIRMED (watch)~Katayama repeats US coordination and readiness to act (no action); BoJ December hike still expected; raw −0.9 held at 0.|WEAKENED (watch)~VIX closed 24.56 on 18 Nov, back below the 25 trigger met on 17 Nov; HY spread 3.43%; regime re-measured on Friday.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Brent settled $91.95 (−1.3%), WTI $83.26 (−3.2%); no verified US–Canada deal; raw −4.5 held at −4.|INVALIDATED (bearish direction)~October jobs −43.5k vs +25k, unemployment 4.9% vs 4.7%: weakness condition met; shadow +3 central, 0 if R2 turns −1.|CONFIRMED~No NZ release or RBNZ remark after T0.|CONFIRMED (fragile)~Gold factor −3 unchanged; gold settled $3,728.80 (−3.5%), price context only; XAU shadow = G + USD shadow.")
